      "3474"
    ]
  },
  {
    "statement": "The effects on species abundance from grooves combined with small protrusions, ridges, or ledges were mixed.",
    "citations": [
      "3474"
    ]
  },
  {
    "statement": "Pit habitats, which are 1-50 mm depressions, increased species richness in a majority of studies.",
    "citations": [
      "3475"
    ]
  },
  {
    "statement": "Higher densities of pit habitats increased the abundance of certain species like periwinkles.",
    "citations": [
      "3475"
    ]
  },
  {
    "statement": "Small ridges or ledges, which are 1-50 mm elevations, had mixed effects on species richness.",
    "citations": [
      "3464"
    ]
  },
  {
    "statement": "Some studies found positive effects on species richness from small ridges or ledges when combined with multiple habitat types.",
    "citations": [
      "3464"
    ]
  },
  {
    "statement": "Large ridges or ledges greater than 50 mm showed consistent positive effects on biodiversity.",
    "citations": [
      "3465"
    ]
  },
  {
    "statement": "Large ridges or ledges increased species diversity and overall macroalgae, microalgae and invertebrate abundance.",
    "citations": [
      "3465"
    ]
  },
  {
    "statement": "The effects of creating linear depressions and elevations on biodiversity varied significantly depending on factors such as habitat size and arrangement, shore level, and site characteristics.",
    "citations": []
  },
  {
    "statement": "The effects of creating linear depressions and elevations on biodiversity also varied depending on the presence of additional habitat features like water-retaining covers or environmentally-sensitive materials.",
    "citations": []
  },
  {
    "statement": "Many studies documented that habitats created with linear depressions and elevations supported species that were absent from flat artificial structure surfaces.",
    "citations": []
  }
]

[
  {
    "statement": "The most consistently effective interventions for altering overall community composition on subtidal artificial structures involve habitat modification approaches.",
    "citations": []
  },
  {
    "statement": "Creating hole habitats larger than 50 mm on subtidal artificial structures consistently altered community composition.",
    "citations": []
  },
  {
    "statement": "The intervention of creating holes, particularly when combined with other features like grooves, environmentally-sensitive materials, and small ledges, consistently changed the combined macroalgae and invertebrate community composition on and around structures.",
    "citations": []
  },
  {
    "statement": "The intervention of creating holes, particularly when combined with other features like grooves, environmentally-sensitive materials, and small ledges, consistently changed the mobile invertebrate and fish community composition on and around structures.",
    "citations": []
  },
  {
    "statement": "Using environmentally-sensitive materials showed consistent effectiveness in altering community composition.",
    "citations": []
  },
  {
    "statement": "Using materials such as shell-concrete, quarried rock, or ECOncreteTM in place of standard-concrete altered the combined macroalgae and invertebrate community composition.",
    "citations": []
  },
  {
    "statement": "Environmentally-sensitive materials consistently changed community composition when combined with texture, grooves, holes, pits, and/or small ledges.",
    "citations": []
  },
  {
    "statement": "Creating pit habitats between 1–50 mm, when combined with holes, grooves, and environmentally-sensitive material, consistently altered community composition.",
    "citations": []
  },
  {
    "statement": "Creating groove habitats between 1–50 mm consistently altered community composition when combined with other habitat features.",
    "citations": []
  },
  {
    "statement": "Creating textured surfaces of 1 mm or less showed mixed results for altering community composition.",
    "citations": []
  },
  {
    "statement": "Creating long flexible habitats larger than 50 mm supported different species assemblages compared to structures without flexible habitats.",
    "citations": []
  },
  {
    "statement": "Ceasing or altering maintenance activities showed less consistent effects on community composition.",
    "citations": []
  },
  {
    "statement": "One study found no alteration of community composition after ceasing or altering maintenance activities.",
    "citations": []
  }
]

